<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the TOP-LEVEL app bundle (Molio 2.0 Editor.app itself),
  applied by electron-builder during macOS code signing because this file
  is named exactly "entitlements.mac.plist" and lives in the configured
  buildResources directory (`build/`, see electron-builder.yml).

  Companion file "entitlements.mac.inherit.plist" in this same folder is
  applied to every OTHER file inside the bundle (helper binaries, the
  MCP server's Node executable, native .node modules) - see that file's
  own comments for why it needs (almost) the same entitlements.

  Kept intentionally minimal: each entry below is a real permission
  granted to the app, so only what Electron itself requires under the
  hardened runtime is listed. Do not add more without a concrete reason
  written down here, and write the reason.

  Background: docs/GitHub-issue references electron-builder itself uses
  for these two exact entries in its own built-in default template
  (node_modules/app-builder-lib/templates/entitlements.mac.plist) - this
  file makes that default explicit and reviewable instead of implicit.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      Electron's main process runs V8 (the Chromium JS engine), and V8
      JIT-compiles JavaScript to native code at runtime. macOS's hardened
      runtime blocks that by default (it normally only allows pages that
      were mapped from a signed file on disk to be executable). Without
      this entitlement the app crashes on startup.
      Ref: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/code-signing
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Same root cause as allow-jit: V8 (and Node's own V8 instance in the
      bundled MCP server binary) allocates executable memory pages that
      were not read from a signed file - needed for its JIT and for
      WebAssembly. Without this, JS execution fails at runtime even
      though the app itself launches.
      Ref: https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/code-signing
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
